How has the Supreme Court's interpretation of the Second Amendment evolved over time, particularly in landmark cases such as District of Columbia v. Heller (2008) and McDonald v. City of Chicago (2010)?
The Supreme Court's interpretation of the Second Amendment has evolved over time, shifting from a collective right model to an individual rights model. This evolution began with the Fifth Circuit's ruling in United States v. Emerson (2001), and was further solidified by the Supreme Court's rulings in District of Columbia v. Heller (2008) and McDonald v. City of Chicago (2010). 

In District of Columbia v. Heller, the Court held that the Second Amendment protects an individual's right to possess a firearm for traditionally lawful purposes, such as self-defense within the home. 

In McDonald v. City of Chicago, the Court extended the individual rights model by holding that the 2nd and 14th amendments to the U.S. constitution were "fully incorporated," and thus the right to keep and bear arms applies to the states.
