What was the National Basketball Association (NBA) salary cap structure in 2017, including any relevant rules and exceptions?
In 2017, the National Basketball Association (NBA) salary cap was set at $94.14 million, with the luxury tax limit at $113.29 million. The salary cap structure in 2017 was a "soft" cap, allowing teams to exceed the cap to retain the rights to a player already on the team, under the "Larry Bird" exception. 

Additionally, there was an exception to the rule that a player's new contract could not exceed the league limit. A player could sign a contract for 105% of their previous contract, even if the new contract was higher than the league limit. 

The 2017 Collective Bargaining Agreement (CBA) also made a change to the determination of maximum salaries, eliminating the difference between the cap calculation and the maximum salary calculation, both of which were based on 44.74% of the league's basketball-related income (BRI).
