Saint Jude is the patron saint associated with lost causes. He is also known as Thaddeus in some versions of the New Testament. In the Roman Catholic Church, Saint Jude is revered for his role in desperate cases and lost causes.

Historically, Saint Jude's association with lost causes dates back to the 19th century in Melanesia, where Christian clergy credited him with ending epidemics such as the measles outbreak of 1860. In the Roman Catholic Church, he is often invoked by those seeking solutions to their problems, including students, people suffering from diseases, and those seeking jobs or family assistance.

In various cultures, Saint Jude's significance extends beyond his association with lost causes. In the Armenian Apostolic Church, he is honored as a patron saint along with Saint Bartholomew. In the Roman Catholic Church, he is also the patron saint of the police force in Chicago, adopted in 1982.
