What is the history of the song 'I Put a Spell on You' and its evolution over time?
The song 'I Put a Spell on You' was written and composed by Jalacy "Screamin' Jay" Hawkins in 1956. It was initially his own recording, which became a classic cult song and his greatest commercial success, reportedly surpassing a million copies in sales. The song was selected as one of the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame's 500 Songs that Shaped Rock and Roll and ranked No. 313 on Rolling Stone magazine's list of The 500 Greatest Songs of All Time.

The song's theatrical act was one of the first shock rock performances, and it has been covered extensively by other artists, with several hundred versions available. Hawkins' own version never charted, but several later cover versions have. The song's evolution over time can be seen in the various covers, with most treating the song seriously and few attempting to duplicate Hawkins' bravura performance.

The song's history is also notable for its influence on the use of screaming vocals in music. It is considered one of the first known songs to utilize screaming vocals, and it has been cited as an example of the use of shouted vocals in blues music. The song's impact can be seen in the work of later artists who have covered the song and in the development of shock rock and experimental music.
