What is the historical origin and evolution of the phrase 'When in Rome, do as the Romans'?
The historical origin of the phrase 'When in Rome, do as the Romans do' is attributed to Saint Ambrose. According to the context, Saint Ambrose advised Saint Monica and her son, Saint Augustine, to follow the conventions of the area they were residing or visiting. He used the example of fasting on Saturdays, stating that when he was in Rome, he fasted on Saturdays, but when he was in Milan, he did not.

The phrase is said to have originated from the following quote by Saint Ambrose: "When I am at Rome, I fast on a Saturday; when I am at Milan, I do not. Follow the custom of the church where you are."

Over time, the phrase evolved from 'When in Rome, do as the Romans do' to 'When in Rome, do as the Pope does', emphasizing the importance of following the conventions of the local authority, in this case, the Pope.
