What was the initial purpose and goals of the Linux kernel project, and how did it evolve over time?
Based on the provided context, the initial purpose of the Linux kernel project was to create a new free operating system kernel for Linus Torvalds' personal computer, with no cross-platform intentions. However, it rapidly attracted developers and users who adopted it as the kernel for other free software projects, notably the GNU Operating System.

The Linux kernel project evolved over time to support a huge array of computer architectures, many more than other operating systems or kernels. It grew from a small number of C files under a license prohibiting commercial distribution to the 4.15 version in 2018 with more than 23.3 million lines of source code under the GNU General Public License.

In summary, the initial goal of the Linux kernel project was to create a personal operating system kernel, but it evolved into a widely adopted and supported kernel for various free software projects, expanding its capabilities and user base over time.
