Identify the origins and historical context of the days of the week
The origins and historical context of the days of the week can be traced back to ancient civilizations, specifically the Babylonians, Greeks, and Romans. 

The seven-day cycle, which is the basis for the modern week, was first practiced in Judaism in the 6th century BC. There are several hypotheses concerning its origin, including the theory that it is approximately a quarter of a lunation (a lunar cycle). The Babylonian calendar used intercalary days to synchronize the last week of a month with the new moon.

The days of the week were originally named after the classical planets, with the Greco-Roman gods associated with these planets being rendered in their "interpretatio germanica". The names of the days of the week were coined in the Roman era, with the Latin names being translated into various languages.

The ISO 8601 standard defines Monday as the first day of the week, derived from Old English "Mōnandæg" and Middle English "Monenday", which is a translation of Latin "dies lunae" or "day of the Moon".
