Examine the impact of the US prime rate on adjustable-rate mortgages and other variable rate short-term loans, and how it affects household debt and economic growth.
The U.S. prime rate has a significant impact on adjustable-rate mortgages (ARM) and other variable rate short-term loans. 

When the prime rate increases, the interest rates on these loans also rise, leading to higher monthly payments for borrowers. This can cause households to default on their mortgages in record numbers, as seen during the Great Recession of 2007-2008. 

The increased debt burden can lead to a deeper recession and a weaker recovery, as households reduce their spending and savings. This, in turn, can affect economic growth. 

In the context of the subprime mortgage crisis, the prime rate played a role in the increased household debt levels, as borrowers took on more debt to maintain living standards. When the prime rate rose, it led to higher interest rates on adjustable-rate mortgages, causing mortgage delinquencies to soar. 

In general, the U.S. prime rate runs approximately 300 basis points (or 3 percent) above the federal funds rate, which is set by the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC). This means that changes in the prime rate can have a ripple effect on the broader economy, impacting household debt and economic growth.
