What is the historical background and significance of designating a National Capital Territory in India?
The historical background of designating a National Capital Territory in India is rooted in the creation of the National Capital Region (NCR) in 1985. The NCR was established to plan the development of the region and evolve harmonized policies for land-use control and infrastructure development. 

However, the significance of designating a National Capital Territory specifically lies in the establishment of the National Capital Territory of Delhi (NCT) as a special Union Territory in 1956. This was done to provide a federally administered area for the capital city, which was previously a part of the Delhi Province. The NCT was later given a legislative assembly in 1991, allowing it to have a degree of autonomy and self-governance.
