What are the pharyngeal gill arches and their embryological origins in vertebrates?
The pharyngeal arches, also known as visceral arches or branchial arches in fish, are structures seen in the embryonic development of vertebrates that are recognisable precursors for many structures. In vertebrates, the pharyngeal arches are derived from all three germ layers (ectoderm, endoderm, and mesoderm) and appear as a series of outpouchings of mesoderm on both sides of the developing pharynx during the fourth week of development in the human embryo.
