Explain the context and significance of the 'Look East' policy during the prime minister's tenure.
The 'Look East' policy was initiated in 1991 during the prime minister's tenure of P.V. Narasimha Rao (1991-1996). It aimed to cultivate extensive economic and strategic relations with Southeast Asian nations to bolster India's standing as a regional power and counterbalance the influence of China.

The policy was further pursued by successive administrations of Atal Bihari Vajpayee (1998-2004) and Manmohan Singh (2004-2014). The 'Look East' policy focused on forging close economic and commercial ties, increasing strategic and security cooperation, and emphasizing historic cultural and ideological links with Southeast Asian nations.

The significance of the 'Look East' policy lies in its strategic shift in India's perspective of the world, moving away from Cold War-era policies and activities. It marked a significant effort to strengthen India's regional influence and position it as a major player in the region.
