What is the history of the North American Indigenous Games, and what is the significance of the event?
The history of the North American Indigenous Games dates back to the 1970s, when the dream to hold a multi-sport event for Indigenous Peoples of North America began. The first Native Summer Games were held in 1971 in Enoch, Alberta, Canada, with 3,000 participants competing in 13 sports and cultural events. 

The Western Canada Native Winter Games were held in 1973. The idea of the North American Indigenous Games became a reality in 1990, when the first NAIG were held in Edmonton, Alberta. Since then, the Games have been held intermittently in various locations across Canada and the United States, including Prince Albert, Saskatchewan, Blaine, Minnesota, Victoria, British Columbia, Winnipeg, Manitoba, Denver, Colorado, Cowichan, British Columbia, and Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

The significance of the North American Indigenous Games lies in its purpose of bringing together Indigenous athletes to excel in their athletic endeavors, as well as to foster new friendships, renew old ones, and promote cultural exchange. As stated by Charles Wood, the 1990 Chairperson, the Games provide a platform for Aboriginal people to come together and celebrate their athletic achievements, cultural heritage, and community spirit.
