Research the historical context and relevant publications related to the discovery of the four largest moons of Jupiter, incorporating the findings from Simon Marius's discovery.
Simon Marius independently discovered the four largest moons of Jupiter (Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto) around the same time as Galileo Galilei in January 1610. However, Marius did not publish his discovery until 1614 in his book "Mundus Iovialis." 

Galileo Galilei, on the other hand, first observed the moons in January 1610 and recognized them as satellites of Jupiter in March 1610. He published his findings in his book "Sidereus Nuncius" in March 1610.

The dispute between Galileo and Marius arose when Galileo accused Marius of plagiarism in his book "Il Saggiatore" in 1623, claiming that Marius had copied his work. However, a jury in The Netherlands in 1903 examined the evidence extensively and found that Marius had indeed made his discovery independently of Galileo.
