Explain the structure and function of adipose tissue in the human body, including its role in energy storage and metabolism.
Based on the provided context, adipose tissue is a loose connective tissue composed mostly of adipocytes, which store energy in the form of lipids. Its main role is to store energy, although it also cushions and insulates the body. In addition to adipocytes, adipose tissue contains the stromal vascular fraction (SVF) of cells including preadipocytes, fibroblasts, vascular endothelial cells, and a variety of immune cells such as adipose tissue macrophages.

There are two types of adipose tissue: white adipose tissue (WAT), which stores energy, and brown adipose tissue (BAT), which generates body heat. Adipose tissue is involved in energy storage and metabolism, with the stored lipids serving as a source of energy when needed. It also produces hormones such as leptin, estrogen, resistin, and the cytokine TNFα, which play roles in various physiological processes.

In humans, adipose tissue is located in various parts of the body, including beneath the skin (subcutaneous fat), around internal organs (visceral fat), in bone marrow (yellow bone marrow), intermuscular, and in the breast tissue. Overall, adipose tissue plays a crucial role in energy storage and metabolism, and its dysfunction has been linked to various diseases, including obesity and metabolic disorders.
